 ### Abel Transform
-The specific case of the Cylindrical Symmetry Projector for parallel-beam geometries. It is chosen when the object is strictly cylindrically symmetric and the acquisition geometry is parallel. See [Abel Transform](abel-transform.md).
+The specific mathematical operation applied in parallel-beam geometries. It is the core of the Cylindrical Symmetry Projector. Choose it when the object is strictly axisymmetric and the projection geometry is parallel. Link: [Abel Transform](abel-transform.md).
+
+### Radial Discretization
+The method of dividing the radial profile into concentric rings. Choose finer discretization for higher accuracy but increased computational cost. Link: [Radial Discretization](radial-discretization.md).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_r \cdot N_z \cdot N_r)$ for naive integration, or $O(N_r \cdot N_z \log N_r)$ with FFT-based methods [G]. Significantly faster than general 3D forward projection ($O(N_r^2 \cdot N_z)$ or higher depending on ray casting).
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_r \cdot N_z)$ for the symmetric volume representation, compared to $O(N_r^2 \cdot N_z)$ for a full 3D volume.
-*   **Strengths:** High computational efficiency for symmetric objects; reduced memory footprint.
-*   **Limitations:** Only applicable to objects with perfect cylindrical symmetry; sensitive to symmetry violations; singularities in integration require careful numerical handling.
-*   **When to use:** Tomographic reconstruction or projection of objects like cylinders, pipes, or biological structures with rotational symmetry.
-*   **When not to use:** Asymmetric objects or complex geometries without symmetry.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_x N_r)$ for the forward projection if the kernel matrix is precomputed. The back projection (inversion) depends on the solver used, typically $O(N_r^2)$ or $O(N_r \log N_r)$ with fast algorithms.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_x N_r)$ to store the kernel matrix $K$.
+*   **Strengths:** Extremely efficient for axisymmetric objects; reduces 3D problem to 1D.
+*   **Limitations:** Only applicable to objects with cylindrical symmetry; sensitive to noise in the input data due to the ill-posed nature of the inverse Abel transform.
+*   **When to use:** When the object is known to be axisymmetric and high performance is required.
+*   **When not to use:** For arbitrary 3D objects or objects with significant asymmetry.
 
 ## Background
-The Cylindrical Symmetry Projector and the Abel Transform have roots in classical tomography and spectroscopy. The Abel Transform is historically used in plasma physics and astrophysics to reconstruct spherically or cylindrically symmetric distributions from line-of-sight projections [S1, S2, unofficial]. Recent implementations in differentiable programming frameworks, such as the one by Champley & Maddox (2021), have integrated these projectors into differentiable forward projector pipelines for X-ray CT, enabling gradient-based optimization for symmetric objects [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The Cylindrical Symmetry Projector, specifically the Abel Transform, has a long history in physics and engineering, particularly in plasma physics and combustion diagnostics [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It was implemented as part of a differentiable forward projector for X-ray CT, allowing for gradient-based optimization in tomographic reconstruction [S2, S3, unofficial]. The discretization approach described here follows the work of Champley & Maddox (2021) [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
